 # DART (Distance-Aware Replication on Tangent-planes)
 ## Overview
-DART is a pre-processing initialization method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that enriches a sparse Structure-from-Motion (SfM) point cloud by generating additional samples on local tangent planes [S1]. It addresses the problem of sparse seeding in scene peripheries and low-texture regions by assigning disk radii that grow with the point’s distance from a robust scene center [S1]. Unlike dynamic densification strategies that spawn primitives during training based on view-space gradients, DART acts once before optimization using only the initial SfM geometry, making it independent of the backbone’s densification dynamics [S1].
+DART is a pre-processing initialization policy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that enriches a sparse Structure-from-Motion (SfM) point cloud by generating additional samples on local tangent planes [S1]. It solves the problem of sparse seeding in scene peripheries and low-texture regions, which typically leads to poor reconstruction quality in those areas [S1]. Unlike dynamic densification methods that spawn primitives during training based on view-space gradients, DART acts once before optimization using only the initial SfM geometry, making it independent of the backbone’s densification dynamics [S1]. Its distinguishing feature is distance-aware replication: the radius of the tangent disk grows with the point’s distance from the scene center, providing wider spread where the SfM cloud is naturally sparser [S1].
 
